## The idea

English reads numbers in **groups of three digits**: "twelve thousand", "three hundred forty five". Write one helper for 1–999, then take the number three digits at a time from the right, adding "Thousand", "Million" or "Billion" — and **skip any group that is zero**.

## Step by step

1. 0 → "Zero".
2. While num > 0: chunk = num % 1000.
3. If chunk ≠ 0: words for the chunk + its scale word, put in front.
4. num /= 1000; next scale.
5. Helper: hundreds digit + "Hundred", then 20–90 from Tens, then 1–19 from Ones.

## Worked example

**Input:** num = 1,000,010

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| chunk 010 | "Ten" |
| chunk 000 | zero → skip (no "Thousand") |
| chunk 1 | "One Million" |

**Result:** "One Million Ten"

## Cost

O(1) — at most four chunks.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 273 — write a non-negative int in English words.
public static class EnglishNumber {
    private static readonly string[] Ones = { "", "One", "Two", "Three", "Four", "Five", "Six", "Seven", "Eight", "Nine",
        "Ten", "Eleven", "Twelve", "Thirteen", "Fourteen", "Fifteen", "Sixteen", "Seventeen", "Eighteen", "Nineteen" };
    private static readonly string[] Tens = { "", "", "Twenty", "Thirty", "Forty", "Fifty", "Sixty", "Seventy", "Eighty", "Ninety" };
    private static readonly string[] Scales = { "", "Thousand", "Million", "Billion" };

    public static string Say(int num) {
        if (num < 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(num));
        if (num == 0) return "Zero";
        var parts = new List<string>();
        int scale = 0;
        while (num > 0) {                                  // take three digits at a time, from the right
            int chunk = num % 1000;
            if (chunk != 0) {                              // 1,000,010: the empty "thousand" chunk says nothing
                string words = Under1000(chunk);
                if (Scales[scale] != "") words += " " + Scales[scale];
                parts.Insert(0, words);                    // at most four chunks, so inserting at 0 is fine
            }
            num /= 1000;
            scale++;
        }
        return string.Join(" ", parts);
    }

    private static string Under1000(int n) {               // 1..999
        var words = new List<string>();
        if (n >= 100) { words.Add(Ones[n / 100]); words.Add("Hundred"); n %= 100; }
        if (n >= 20) { words.Add(Tens[n / 10]); n %= 10; }
        if (n > 0) words.Add(Ones[n]);                     // 1..19 have their own words
        return string.Join(" ", words);
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Where does it usually break?**

Zero chunks (1,000,010), the teens, and 0 itself.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(1) — at most four chunks.

**Q: Shared state?**

Static read-only word tables.

**Q: Other languages.**

Indian English groups lakh and crore (2 digits after the first 3) — the chunking becomes a table, not code.

**Q: How would you test it?**

The boundary numbers: 0, 10–20, 99, 100, 101, 999, 1000, 1001, 1,000,000, int.MaxValue.
